              "id": "glog-17e56505-6341-4d66-afe4-08966905e8c2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Exec Usage Detected\" is a vulnerability in Python programming language that arises when the `exec()` function is used. The `exec()` function is a built-in Python function that executes the Python code contained within a string or object. This function can be a security risk because it can execute arbitrary Python code. If an attacker can control the input to the `exec()` function, they can execute arbitrary commands, leading to remote code execution (RCE) vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `exec()` function whenever possible. If you must use it, never use it on user-supplied input or other untrusted data. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not execute code dynamically, such as using functions or classes.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `exec()`, consider using safer alternatives. For example, if you're using `exec()` to call a function, you could use a dictionary of functions instead:\n\n```python\ndef function1():\n    print(\"Function 1\")\n\ndef function2():\n    print(\"Function 2\")\n\nfunctions = {\n    'function1': function1,\n    'function2': function2\n}\n\n# Instead of exec(\"function1()\"), use:\nfunctions['function1']()\n```\n\nIn this example, the dictionary `functions` maps strings to function objects. Instead of using `exec()` to execute a function based on its name, you can look up the function in the dictionary and call it directly.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
